1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,200
Something I want to speak on is the value of extending love, not for the sake of the

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,660
person having to respond, but for the understanding that when you send it, it is received regardless.

3
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:18,020
Now, what I mean about that is in the specific instance of when you know someone has had

4
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:25,160
a loss or someone has gone through something traumatic or just any time that you know that

5
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:31,960
somebody may be in a situation where they could use some love. I think a lot of times we tell

6
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:38,520
ourselves they want privacy or they need space or, you know, they don't want to be bothered.

7
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:48,580
And then we will actually not reach out. I remember when I miscarried and I had told a friend

8
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:52,880
and they just never, they like said one thing to me and then I never heard from them again.

9
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,700
And I addressed it later and they said, Oh, well, I just thought you needed space. I said,

10
00:00:56,740 --> 00:01:00,180
but I didn't ask for space. And they were like, Oh, well, I just thought that's what you needed.

11
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:05,060
But I think what it really happens is that a lot of us, you know, we are afraid of our emotions and

12
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:10,180
we are not in touch with the way that compassion when it's needed makes us feel. And then sometimes

13
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:15,840
we're all so busy, but we have to be conscious enough to know that the act of reaching out

14
